       <div class="ch">
<h1>Relaxation oscillator with a comparator</h1>

<div class="ch-card">
<h2>How it works</h2>
<ul>
<li>The output <code>out</code> swings between <strong>Vol = −5 V</strong> and <strong>Voh = +5 V</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>R</strong> and <strong>R2</strong> bring a fraction β = R2/(R + R2) = 0.5 of the output back to the non-inverting input <code>p</code> (positive feedback): the threshold is +2.5 V when the output is high and −2.5 V when it is low.</li>
<li><strong>R1</strong> charges <strong>C1</strong> from the output (negative feedback). When the voltage <code>n</code> of the capacitor crosses the threshold on <code>p</code>, the output flips and C1 starts to charge in the other direction.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Frequency</h2>
<ul>
<li><code>f = 1 / (2 · R1 · C1 · ln((1 + β)/(1 − β)))</code> = 1 / (2 · 10 kΩ · 100 nF · ln 3) ≈ 455 Hz (about 2.2 ms).</li>
<li>In the PyAMS simulation the period is 2.2 ms. The first period is longer because the capacitor starts discharged.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Notes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Not the usual drawing:</strong> in the picture of a relaxation oscillator that is often shown, <code>R</code> comes from the input terminal. Here <code>R</code> comes from the <strong>output</strong>, because without positive feedback there are no two thresholds and the circuit cannot oscillate.</li>
<li><strong>Vol = −5 V:</strong> with the default levels (0 V and 5 V) the lower threshold is 0 V and the capacitor, which discharges toward 0 V, never reaches it: the oscillation stops.</li>
<li><strong>Vos = 1 mV:</strong> the circuit is perfectly symmetric and would stay at 0 V; a small input offset gives the start (like the noise of a real circuit).</li>
<li>Time domain analysis: 0 ... 10 ms with a step of 5 µs. Plotted: <code>out</code>, the capacitor <code>n</code> and the threshold <code>p</code>.</li>
</ul>
</div>

    </div>
    </div></foreignObject></g>